//
// AN OPERATOR ON A BUILT-IN TYPE THE STDLIB HAS A METHOD FOR SAYS ONLY THE REFUSAL
// (`@FR-Op-Home`): `operator negate(self: float)` is on a built-in type and is refused; the stdlib
// already holds a `negate` on `float`, which makes it a collision too, but the program has one
// mistake and its diagnostics must say one — never a syntax error against a well-formed
// signature.  One refusal per file.
//
// @falsified-at: 18594597b (loft#2086) — interpret and native: the claimed error plus "Cannot redefine
//   'negate'" and "Syntax error: unexpected '->'".
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic, and no other).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the type.
// HOLDS: an operator name the stdlib does NOT hold on the type (`times` on `boolean`) draws the
//   Op-Home refusal alone (the sibling cell).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `negate` is a stdlib method on `float`

operator negate(self: float) -> float { self }

fn main() {
  println("x");
}
